transport.latex_model:
Let $D=42$ t be the demand, $R=\frac{10\,000}{1000}=10$ t the per-launch capacity, $E_{\max}=25$ t the elevator capacity, and $F=11000$ the fixed launch cost. For each integer launch count $n\ge0$, the least feasible elevator tonnage is
\begin{equation}
e_n=\max\{0,\;D-nR\},\qquad e_n\le E_{\max}.
\end{equation}
The cost of the corresponding schedule is
\begin{equation}
C_n=c\,e_n+F\,n.
\end{equation}
Only $n=2,3,4,5$ are feasible: $(n,e_n)=(2,22)$ with $C=22c+22000$; $(3,12)$ with $C=12c+33000$; $(4,2)$ with $C=2c+44000$; and $(5,0)$ with $C=55000$. The optimal plan switches where the three middle plans tie:
\begin{equation}
22c+22000=12c+33000,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
12c+33000=2c+44000.
\end{equation}
Both hold at $c=1100$, giving the shared cost $22(1100)+22000=46200$. At the nominal price $c=800$ the optimum is $(e,n)=(22,2)$ with cost $39600$; at $c=650$ it remains $(22,2)$ with cost $36300$; at $c=1500$ it becomes $(2,4)$ with cost $47000$.

inference.latex_model:
For contestant $A$ with judge share $j_A$ and fan share $p_A$,
\begin{equation}
s_A=\alpha j_A+(1-\alpha)p_A,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
s_B=\alpha(1-j_A)+(1-\alpha)(1-p_A).
\end{equation}
Strict combined-score ranking means $s_A>s_B$, with
\begin{equation}
s_A-s_B=\alpha(2j_A-1)+(1-\alpha)(2p_A-1)>0 .
\end{equation}
When $\alpha<1$ this is equivalent to
\begin{equation}
p_A>\frac{\frac12-\alpha j_A}{1-\alpha},
\end{equation}
while $\alpha=1$ gives $2j_A-1>0$, independent of $p_A$.
Week~1 uses $j_A=0.9$, $\alpha=0.5$, $s_A=0.6$, so $0.6=0.5(0.9)+0.5\,p_A$ and $p_A=0.3$. For the base rank week the feasibility set is $p_A>0.1$, i.e. $p_A\in(0.1,\,1]$. Extra cases: $j_A=1$, $\alpha=0.5$ gives $p_A>0$; $j_A=0$, $\alpha=0.5$ gives $p_A>1$, empty on $[0,1]$; $j_A=0.8$, $\alpha=1$ gives every $p_A\in[0,1]$.
